{{also|Cadence|cadencé}}
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ḱh₂d-}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|frm|cadence}}, from {{der|en|it-oit|cadenza|t=conclusion of a phrase of music}}, from {{der|en|la|*cadentia|lit=a falling}}, form of {{m|la|cadēns}}, the present participle of {{m|la|cadō|t=I fall, I cease}}. The Latin verb is inherited, via {{der|en|itc-pro|*kadō}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*ḱad-|alt=*ḱad-e-|t=to fall|pos=thematic present}}. {{doublet|en|cadenza|chance}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkeɪ.dn̩s/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-cadence.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The act or state of [[decline|declining]] or [[sink]]ing.
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=10|passage=Now was the sun in western '''cadence''' low.}}
# The [[measure]] or [[beat]] of movement.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1993|volume=98|issue=5|page=102|title=Terror of the deep|author=Ken Schultz|journal=Field and Stream|passage=Getting into a good jigging rhythm means making short quick jerks in a regular '''cadence''' that might average about one jerk every 1.5 to 2 seconds.}}
# [[balance|Balanced]], [[rhythmic]] flow.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Love's Labour's Lost|act=IV|scene=ii|passage=You find not the apostrophas, and so miss the accent:<br>let me supervise the canzonet. Here are only numbers ratified;<br>but, for the elegancy, facility, and golden '''cadence''' of poesy,<br>caret.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2 December 1991|title=At the Saudi-Kuwaiti Border|work=ABC Nightline|passage=Night has now passed in the Saudi desert and as we hear from Nightline correspondent Forrest Sawyer, the normal '''cadence''' of life at the front is about to change.}}
# The general inflection or modulation of the voice, or of any sound.
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=2|passage=Blustering winds, which all night long / Had roused the sea, now with hoarse '''cadence''' lull / Seafaring men o'erwatched.}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Guy Mannering|passage=The accents {{...}} were in passion's tenderest '''cadence'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1986| author=w:John le Carré| title=w:A Perfect Spy| passage=Then away at last they sped to the house or bedside of some elderly and worthy person, and Pym sat fascinated to see how swiftly Rick trimmed his manner to suit theirs, how naturally he slipped into the '''cadences''' and vernacular that put them most at ease, and how the love of God came into his good face when he talked about Liberalism and Masonry and his dear dead father, God rest him, and a firstclass rate of return, ten percent guaranteed plus profits for as long as you're spared.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=30 December 1991|title=Raimey: A Breath of Fresh Ayah|author=David Holmstrom|work=Christian Science Monitor|passage=The '''cadence''' of Raimey's voice is pure Down-Easter Maine}}
# {{lb|en|music}} A progression of at least two [[chord|chords]] which conclude a piece of [[music]], section or musical phrases within it. Sometimes referred to [[analogy|analogously]] as musical [[punctuation]].
# {{lb|en|music}} A [[cadenza]], or closing embellishment; a pause before the end of a [[strain]], which the performer may fill with a [[flight of fancy]].
# {{lb|en|speech}} A fall in [[inflection]] of a speaker’s voice, such as at the end of a sentence.
# {{lb|en|dance}} A dance move which ends a phrase.
#: ''The '''cadence''' in a galliard step refers to the final leap in a cinquepace sequence.''
# {{lb|en|fencing}} The rhythm and sequence of a series of actions.
# {{lb|en|running}} The number of steps per minute.
# {{lb|en|cycling}} The number of revolutions per minute of the [[crank]]s or pedals of a [[bicycle]].
# {{lb|en|military}} A chant that is sung by military personnel while running or marching; a [[jody call]].
# {{lb|en|heraldry}} [[cadency|Cadency]].
# {{lb|en|horse-riding}} Harmony and proportion of movement, as in a well-managed [[horse]].
# {{lb|en|horseracing}} The number of strides per second of a racehorse, measured when the same foot/hoof strikes the ground
# {{lb|en|software development}} The [[frequency]] of regular product [[release]]s.
#* {{quote-book|1=en|author=Dean Leffingwell|title=Agile Software Requirements: Lean Requirements Practices for Teams, Programs, and the Enterprise|publisher=Addison-Wesley|year=2010|month=12|page=317|isbn=978-0-321-63584-6|text=In this third case, releasing more frequently, the PSI '''cadence''' becomes a ''planning'' '''cadence''', rather than a ''release'' '''cadence'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|author=w:Scott Ambler; Mark Lines|title=Disciplined Agile Delivery: A Practitioner's Guide to Agile Software Delivery in the Enterprise|publisher=IBM Press|year=2012|page=227|text=We recommend aiming for a release '''cadence''' of no more than six months, with a goal of getting it down to three months or shorter.}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|author=Jaokim Verona; Michael Duffy; Paul Swartout|title=Learning DevOps: Continuously Develop Better Software|publisher=Packt Publishing|year=2016|month=9|page=47|isbn=978-1-78712-661-9|text=This happens when the installation '''cadence''' in production is slower than the release '''cadence''' of the development teams.}}

